  "question": "How do different tokenization strategies and vocabulary sizes affect the performance of large language models across different languages and domains?",
  "llm_response": "Tokenization is fundamental to LLM performance. Byte Pair Encoding (BPE), used by GPT models, iteratively merges frequent character pairs to create subword tokens, balancing vocabulary size with sequence length. WordPiece, used by BERT, similarly creates subwords but uses likelihood-based merging. SentencePiece, used by T5 and LLaMA, operates on raw text without pre-tokenization, making it language-agnostic. Larger vocabularies reduce sequence length but increase embedding parameters; GPT-2 uses 50K tokens while LLaMA uses 32K. For multilingual models, larger vocabularies help represent diverse scripts, but over-segmentation of non-English text remains a challenge. Domain-specific tokenizers trained on specialized corpora (medical, legal) can significantly improve performance in those domains.",
